athcal S_k\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   Translation preserves parallelism, so every line of \\(\\mathcal S_k'\\) is\n   still sunny, and the lines remain distinct.  By the bijection above,\n   \\(\\mathcal S_k'\\) covers exactly the set \\(T_n'\\).\n\nThe total family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal S_k'\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, satisfies (2), and has precisely the\n\\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k'\\) sunny.  This completes the inductive step.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=n\\) we have \\(n-k=0\\); no vertical lines are allowed.  \nApply the inductive construction for the pair \\((n-1,n-1)\\) to obtain\n\\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \nAdd a further sunny line \\(\\ell\\) through the point \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is\ndifferent from the slopes of the existing \\(n-1\\) lines and is not\nhorizontal, vertical, nor slope \\(-1\\).  \nThe new line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, distinct from the previous ones, and together\nthe \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) (the points with\nfirst coordinate \\(n\\) are exactly \\((n,1)\\)).  Hence the case \\(k=n\\) is\nalso realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Completion of the induction  \n\nThe base cases \\(k=0,1\\) are established, the inductive step handles every\n\\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k<n\\), and the separate argument treats \\(k=n\\).  Therefore,\nfor any integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (3) together with the constructive proof above shows\nthat the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\