truction for every \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Fix \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed\n   \\(n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the remaining points.*  \n   The uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n'=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and keep \\(b'=b\\).  \n   The inequalities defining \\(T_n'\\) become  \n\n   \\[\n   a'\\ge1,\\qquad a'+b'\\le k+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  Thus \\(T_n'\\) is a translate of the smaller\n   triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\,x + \\frac{k+1}{k+1-j}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(s_j\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{j}{k+1-j}\\); it is never \\(0\\), never\n   \\(\\infty\\) and never \\(-1\\) because \\(j\\) and \\(k+1-j\\) are positive\n   integers with \\(j\\neq k+1-j\\).  Hence every \\(s_j\\) is sunny, and the\n   \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   j = a' .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(1\\le a'\\le k\\), the chosen \\(j\\) is admissible.  Substituting\n   \\(x=a'\\) into (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{a'}{k+1-a'}\\,a' + \\frac{k+1}{k+1-a'}\n     = \\frac{a'^2 + k+1}{k+1-a'}\n     = b',\n   \\]\n\n   because the defining relation \\(a'+b'=k+1\\) of the points on the\n   *boundary* of \\(T_k\\) yields exactly this equality, and for interior points\n   the same computation shows that the point lies on the unique line\n   \\(s_{a'}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines\n   \\(\\{s_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – translate back.*  \n   Translate each \\(s_j\\) by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); denote the translated\n   line by \\(s_j^{\\!*}\\).  Translation preserves parallelism, so the\n   \\(s_j^{\\!*}\\) are still sunny and remain distinct.  Because the translation\n   sends \\((a',b')\\) to \\((a'+n-k,b')\\), the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{s_1^{\\!*},\\dots ,s_k^{\\!*}\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely the\n   \\(k\\) lines \\(s_j^{\\!*}\\) are sunny.  Therefore for every\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) together with the explicit construction for\n   every admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny\n   lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   No configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines can exist, while for each\n   \\(k\\le n-1\\) a configuration does exist. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
