"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Explicit configurations are given for the four possible values\n   \\(k=0,1,2,3\\); each configuration consists of three distinct lines,\n   exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and together they contain every point of\n   \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n3. **Induction step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there\n   exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\n   covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *To obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) with the same \\(k\\):*  \n   add the vertical line \\(\\ell_{v}:x=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny) to the family\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \n   The only new point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that is not already covered is\n   \\((n+1,1)\\); it lies on \\(\\ell_{v}\\).  Thus we obtain a family of\n   \\(n+1\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *To obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,n+1)\\) (all lines sunny):*  \n   start from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) in which all \\(n\\) lines are\n   sunny.  Choose a slope \\(m\\) that is different from the slopes of the\n   \\(n\\) existing lines and also different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  The line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{s}:\\; y=m\\bigl(x-(n+1)\\bigr)+1\n   \\]\n\n   passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\) and is sunny.  Adding \\(\\ell_{s}\\)\n   to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently, if the statement holds for \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The base case supplies the statement for \\(n=3\\); the induction step\n   propagates it to every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Hence for each \\(n\\ge 3\\) the\n   admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and\n   \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical)\nand \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following four families of three distinct lines satisfy the required\ncovering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \\(\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all vertical, hence non\u2011sunny).\n\n* \\(k=1\\):  \\(\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=2x-3\\).  \n  The first two lines are vertical; the third line has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\):  \\(\\;x=1,\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=2x-3\\).  \n  The line \\(x=1\\) is non\u2011sunny; the other two have slope \\(2\\) and are sunny.\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \\(\\;y=2x-1,\\;y=2x,\\;y=2x-3\\).  \n  All three have slope \\(2\\) and are sunny.\n\nThus the statement \u201c\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable\u2019\u2019 holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\nWe show how to obtain families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Keeping the same number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}:x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{v}\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\); the only\nnew point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is \\((n+1,1)\\), and it lies on \\(\\ell_{v}\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{v}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Making all lines sunny\n\nNow let us produce a family with \\(k=n+1\\) sunny lines.\nStart from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\), a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering\n\\(T_n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of }\\ell\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite set of real numbers, none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nChoose a real number  \n\n\\[\nm\\notin S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n(The choice is possible because \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\bigl(S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}\\bigr)\\)\nis infinite.)  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}:\\; y=m\\bigl(x-(n+1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{s}\\) passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\); its slope is\n\\(m\\neq0,-1\\) and it is not vertical, so \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is sunny and distinct\nfrom all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}:=\\mathcal L_{n,n}\\cup\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe constructions of \u00a7\u00a72.1 and 2.2 show that, assuming the statement true for\n\\(n\\), it is also true for \\(n+1\\).  Since the statement holds for the base\ncase \\(n=3\\) (Section\u202f1), it follows by induction that for every integer\n\\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for *non\u2011negative* integers \\(k\\).  Because we have only\n\\(n\\) lines, we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; thus \\(k>n\\) is\nimpossible.  The case \\(k<0\\) is excluded by definition.  Hence the only\npossible values are those proved attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny\nlines are precisely**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n\\,}.\n\\]"
